10     What the results show and what they do not
What the two tests support together. At the fixed parameters, the prescribed D2+H3
searches yield a lower measured gain for R3 than for B3 on every sampled market, with R3’s
registered efficiency condition met. The gain is measured against each rule’s frozen truthful refer-
ence; it does not isolate the marginal benefit of being allowed to replace identities, which would
need a matched comparison with replacement disabled. The margin the first test claimed does not
carry over to the whole neighbourhood. The pooled factorial contrasts and the comparison with C∗
support a measured benefit shared by more than one targeting design; they do not establish the
same ordering for every variant on every market. At ρ = 0.2, pacing slightly raises the measured
gain while changing efficiency little; at ρ = 1, the terminal reserve that pacing adds exhausts the
allowance and causes R3’s collapse, which the capped schedule only partly repairs.

What they do not support. The selected unconstrained welfare attacks lower the gross effi-
ciency of all three core rules by a similar amount while earning less than the truthful reference
against R3 and C∗ ; that is a loss relative to honest play, not necessarily a negative profit. Because
the profit-constrained welfare search excludes randomised policies, its favourable result for R3 and
C∗ is not a lower bound on efficiency against attackers who keep their mean gain non-negative.
Robustness to the searched families is not established: the doubling checks find missed improve-
ments but cannot bound the remaining gap, and several decisions hold only for the prescribed search
procedure. The fresh-market configurations differ from the registered ones in the last binary digit;
re-evaluating the frozen policies and references with the registered configurations leaves every Q1
and Q4 decision unchanged, a check that does not repeat search or reference selection.

Exact but costly checks. The model’s audit already reveals delivered quality exactly, at a cost
charged to the operator’s budget. An execution proof such as FlashProve checks a different object,
that a committed computation ran, and does not remove delivery noise. It fits the model’s audit
cost only if the operator funds both proving and verification; provider-funded proving would need
separate incentive accounting. Either way, which jobs to check and how to sanction remain the
questions; if the cost of a proof fell close to that of the computation itself, the case for sampled
audits would weaken. This discussion is outside the registered tests and changes none of their
claims.

Limits. The benchmark is a unilateral deviation against honest opponents; we claim neither
equilibrium nor incentive compatibility. At the first-test parameters, Proposition 1 establishes a
profitable small same-offer shading deviation in a round with zero continuation loss under every
studied rule. Exposure is bounded by the searched families and the optimiser. The fresh markets
come from one stated neighbourhood, and the headline market was used in development.


11     Conclusion
For the uniform-audit baselines with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, free replacement
removes the ban’s continuation cost. At the fixed parameters, the prescribed D2+H3 searches yield
a lower measured gain for R3 than for B3 on every sampled market, but the registered gain-and-
efficiency comparison passes on only three of six fresh markets, all three qualified by search flags. R3
and the development-selected verified-history comparator are pooled-equivalent within the registered
margin under both Q1 families. At the headline market, the selected unconstrained welfare attacks


