 \paragraph{Fresh markets.} The block hash seeds a generator of market instances around the headline market: fixed scale, noise, budget ratio, audit cost, and cost-quality association, with provider ceilings, costs, capacities, and the top job value drawn per instance (Appendix~\ref{app:params}). Six instances and 200 evaluation seeds per market were drawn from it. The claims concern this neighbourhood, not markets in general.
 
-\paragraph{Decision rules.} Table~\ref{tab:rules-v2} summarises them; the protocol file is the authority. Pooled statistics weight the six markets equally, and intervals use a studentized bootstrap that resamples seeds within each market.
+\paragraph{Decision rules.} Table~\ref{tab:rules-v2} summarises them; the protocol file is the authority. Pooled statistics weight the six markets equally, and intervals use a studentized bootstrap (\const{9999} resamples) that resamples seeds within each market, pairing the rules' outcomes on each seed; this measures uncertainty over seeds in the six fixed markets, not over new markets or repeated searches. The gain is $g = (\pi^{A} - \pi^{T})/(S^*/N)$, attacked profit minus the profit of the rule's frozen truthful reference, normalised by the first-best surplus per provider; with $N = \const{10}$ providers, the Q1 margin of $\const{0.05}$ is half a percent of the first-best surplus. \emph{Calibration.} A multiplier $\kappa$ replaces each nominal tail $\alpha_e$ by $\kappa\alpha_e$, so $\kappa < \const{1}$ widens the intervals. It was selected from $\{1, 1/2, 1/4\}$ on fixtures built from frozen development attacks and references on the headline market and dev0 (pools of \const{20000} seeds per market; Q1 in six equal strata), using \const{10000} selection replications, and admitted on \const{20000} independent validation replications if every endpoint's Clopper--Pearson upper bound on its miss rate, at confidence $1 - \const{0.01}/m$ over the $m$ endpoints of the family, was at most its allocated tail. The fixtures hold the policies and references fixed, so admission does not cover search or reference selection, and it does not establish nominal coverage on the fresh, selected-rule distributions. Q1's categories follow the logic of equivalence testing and Q4's market test is an intersection-union test~\cite{berger1996bioequivalence}; the calibration follows the reporting of simulation studies of statistical methods~\cite{morris2019simulation}. Holm's procedure controls the direction decisions within Q1 and within Q4; the Q1 intervals are calibrated Bonferroni intervals; the efficiency condition is a separate nominal one-sided \const{97.5} percent bound.
 
