-\paragraph{Stronger attacks and where R3 fails (Q3).} Table~\ref{tab:q3-envelope} gives each core rule's attack envelope, the family with the highest training gain, evaluated unchanged. Against B3 the envelope reaches \site{r_q3_f000_f1} on fresh1; against R3 it stays at \site{r_q3_f111_f1} there, and against C$^*$ at \site{r_q3_cs_f1}, from \site{r_q3_cs_f1_fam}. The independently authored, defence-aware family H4, run at the headline market only, gains \site{r_q3_f111_h4} against R3 and \site{r_q3_f000_h4} against B3. Robustness against D2+H3 is unanswered for every core rule because of search flags, and so is R3's robustness against X7-SC; X5-AG/v2 and H4 carry no search-quality claim. Welfare attacks are the clearest weakness: the training-selected attack without an attacker-profit constraint (W0) lowers R3's gross efficiency to \site{r_q3_w0}, as it does for the other core rules, while with the constraint (W1) efficiency stays at \site{r_q3_w1} (Table~\ref{tab:q3-welfare}). Coalitions gain far more against B3 (two synchronised members: \site{r_q3_coal_f000}) than against R3 (five synchronised members: \site{r_q3_coal_f111}); we make no coalition-stability claim (Table~\ref{tab:q3-coalitions}). At $\rho = \const{1}$, where R3's terminal reserve collapses, R3 leaves \site{r_rep_f111}; the repair R4 lowers this to \site{r_rep_r4} and C$^*$ with the capped schedule (C$^*$/S2) to \site{r_rep_cs2} (Table~\ref{tab:q3-repair}).
+\paragraph{Stronger attacks and where R3 fails (Q3).} Table~\ref{tab:q3-envelope} gives each core rule's attack envelope: the family with the highest training gain, evaluated unchanged. These are finite-search results, not bounds on the families. Against B3 the selected envelope reaches \site{r_q3_f000_f1} on fresh1; against R3 it is \site{r_q3_f111_f1} there, and against C$^*$ \site{r_q3_cs_f1}, from \site{r_q3_cs_f1_fam}. H4 was written in a separate session against the frozen defenders and committed by its hash before the lock; its author's containment note states that it contains D2+H3 exactly as a special case, so it extends the public family rather than offering a disjoint attack concept. Evaluated at the headline market only, its selected search gains \site{r_q3_f111_h4} against R3 and \site{r_q3_f000_h4} against B3. Robustness against D2+H3 is unanswered for every core rule because of search flags, and so is R3's robustness against X7-SC; X5-AG/v2 and H4 carry no search-quality claim. Welfare attacks are the clearest weakness. At the headline market, the selected W0 attacks (no profit constraint) yield gross efficiency \site{r_q3_w0_f000} for B3 and \site{r_q3_w0} for R3, with gains relative to the truthful reference of \site{r_q3_w0g_f000} and \site{r_q3_w0g_f111}; C$^*$ is like R3. Under the prescribed W1 searches (mean gain at least zero), the selected feasible attacks yield efficiency \site{r_q3_w1} for R3, while B3 stays at \site{r_q3_w1_f000} (Table~\ref{tab:q3-welfare}). These are not lower bounds: W1 constrains the mean gain, so a lottery that picks a welfare attack or a profitable attack at the start of each episode is also feasible. A post hoc lottery that plays R3's W0 attack with probability \site{r_ph_w_r3} and its H4 attack otherwise keeps the mean gain non-negative (one-sided \const{95} percent lower bound) and lowers R3's efficiency to \site{r_ph_e_r3}; for C$^*$ the corresponding lottery reaches \site{r_ph_e_cs} (Table~\ref{tab:ph-welfare};