 \section{What the results show and what they do not}\label{sec:discussion}
 % BEGIN-EV2
-\paragraph{What the two tests support together.} Against the identity-replacement attacker, targeting new identities lowers the gain that free replacement buys: R3 leaves a lower gain than uniform auditing on every sampled market, with its efficiency condition met. The margin the first test claimed does not carry over to the whole neighbourhood, and the advantage is not specific to R3: a rule that ranks identities by verified history does about as well, and in the factorial either the priority phase or newest-first targeting alone recovers most of the effect. Within the scope of Section~\ref{sec:design}, the finding therefore holds across the tested age-ranked variants and the selected verified-history comparator; it is not specific to R3's particular design. Pacing contributes little at the registered audit cost ($\rho = \const{0.2}$), and the terminal reserve that pacing adds is the source of R3's collapse at high audit cost ($\rho = \const{1}$), which the capped schedule only partly repairs.
+\paragraph{What the two tests support together.} At the fixed parameters, the prescribed D2+H3 searches yield a lower measured gain for R3 than for B3 on every sampled market, with R3's registered efficiency condition met. The gain is measured against each rule's frozen truthful reference; it does not isolate the marginal benefit of being allowed to replace identities, which would need a matched comparison with replacement disabled. The margin the first test claimed does not carry over to the whole neighbourhood. The pooled factorial contrasts and the comparison with C$^*$ support a measured benefit shared by more than one targeting design; they do not establish the same ordering for every variant on every market. At $\rho = \const{0.2}$, pacing slightly raises the measured gain while changing efficiency little; at $\rho = \const{1}$, the terminal reserve that pacing adds exhausts the allowance and causes R3's collapse, which the capped schedule only partly repairs.
 
-\paragraph{What they do not support.} The welfare attack without a profit constraint lowers the efficiency of every core rule by a similar amount, so the targeting rules do not protect welfare against an attacker who will accept losses. Robustness against the strongest searched family is unanswered wherever the doubling check flagged the search, and several decisions hold only for the prescribed search procedure. The fresh-market configurations differ from the registered ones in the last binary digit; a re-evaluation with the registered configurations leaves every Q1 and Q4 decision unchanged (Section~\ref{sec:design}).
+\paragraph{What they do not support.} The selected unconstrained welfare attacks lower the gross efficiency of all three core rules by a similar amount while earning less than the truthful reference against R3 and C$^*$; that is a loss relative to honest play, not necessarily a negative profit. Because the profit-constrained welfare search excludes randomised policies, its favourable result for R3 and C$^*$ is not a lower bound on efficiency against attackers who keep their mean gain non-negative. Robustness to the searched families is not established: the doubling checks find missed improvements but cannot bound the remaining gap, and several decisions hold only for the prescribed search procedure. The fresh-market configurations differ from the registered ones in the last binary digit; re-evaluating the frozen policies and references with the registered configurations leaves every Q1 and Q4 decision unchanged, a check that does not repeat search or reference selection.
 % END-EV2
 \paragraph{Exact but costly checks.} The model's audit already reveals delivered quality exactly, at a cost charged to the operator's budget. An execution proof such as FlashProve checks a different object, that a committed computation ran, and does not remove delivery noise. It fits the model's audit cost only if the operator funds both proving and verification; provider-funded proving would need separate incentive accounting. Either way, which jobs to check and how to sanction remain the questions; if the cost of a proof fell close to that of the computation itself, the case for sampled audits would weaken. This discussion is outside the registered tests and changes none of their claims.
 
-\paragraph{Limits.} The benchmark is a unilateral deviation against honest opponents; we claim neither equilibrium nor incentive compatibility, and small shading inside the tolerance is profitable under every studied rule (Proposition~\ref{prop:terminal}). Exposure is bounded by the searched families and the optimiser. The fresh markets come from one stated neighbourhood, and the headline market was used in development.
+\paragraph{Limits.} The benchmark is a unilateral deviation against honest opponents; we claim neither equilibrium nor incentive compatibility. At the first-test parameters, Proposition~\ref{prop:terminal} establishes a profitable small same-offer shading deviation in a round with zero continuation loss under every studied rule. Exposure is bounded by the searched families and the optimiser. The fresh markets come from one stated neighbourhood, and the headline market was used in development.
 
 \section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}
 % BEGIN-EV2
-Free identity replacement makes a ban costless under uniform audits, and in our tests directing audits at new or unverified identities reduces the measured replacement gain. Two registered tests, the second on fresh markets drawn after the lock, show that this holds in sign across the sampled markets but not with the margin first claimed, that a verified-history rule matches the age-ranked rule we proposed, and that neither protects efficiency against loss-accepting welfare attacks. The record of both tests, including the original failure, the amendments, the deviations, and the failed predictions, is released with the paper.
+For the uniform-audit baselines with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, free replacement removes the ban's continuation cost. At the fixed parameters, the prescribed D2+H3 searches yield a lower measured gain for R3 than for B3 on every sampled market, but the registered gain-and-efficiency comparison passes on only three of six fresh markets, all three qualified by search flags. R3 and the development-selected verified-history comparator are pooled-equivalent within the registered margin under both Q1 families. At the headline market, the selected unconstrained welfare attacks reduce the gross efficiency of every core rule while earning less than the truthful reference, and a post hoc lottery shows that the profit-constrained welfare result depends on excluding randomised policies. These conclusions concern frozen searches and references, with interval calibration admitted on development fixtures. The record of both tests, including the original failure, the amendments, the deviations, and the failed predictions, will be released with the paper.
 % END-EV2
 
 \paragraph{Availability.} The release, at the report commit and signed tag \texttt{protocol-v2}, will contain the code, the tuned files, all records of both tests (including incomplete, failed, and cut cells), the market generator, the block headers, the released H4 bundle, the deviation evidence and the decision-stability re-evaluation, and the paper sources. One command (\texttt{docs/revision/deviation/reproduce\_deviation.py}) checks the artifact against the tag and reproduces the second test's report under the disclosed configuration substitution; the second test's tables and figures are regenerated from that report by the scripts in \texttt{docs/revision/paper-v2}, and the first test's records and verdict are in the release as recorded in October~\const{2026}.

