     2	
     3	Thank you for a careful and useful review. Every sentence correction is applied; the four zero-cost analyses you
     4	proposed were run on the existing records and are reported in the paper as exploratory. Two of them changed the paper's
     5	substance (points A and B below). Section numbers refer to the revised draft attached (`main.pdf`).
     6	
     7	Your review saw only the PDF. This time the materials are attached as separate files, so the report, the addendum, the
     8	protocol, the predictions, the ledger, and H4's code and containment note can be checked directly.
     9	
    10	## What changed in substance
    11	
    12	**A. The welfare result was overstated, as you suspected (your Task 2.5).** W1 constrains the mean gain, so an
    13	episode-level lottery between a welfare attack and a profitable attack is feasible. On the evaluation records, a lottery
    14	that plays R3's W0 attack with probability 0.42 and its H4 attack otherwise keeps the mean gain non-negative (one-sided
    15	95 percent lower bound) and lowers R3's gross efficiency to 0.932, against 0.997 under the registered W1 search; for C*
    16	the lottery reaches 0.921 (`posthoc.json`, Table "ph-welfare"). The paper now says that the profit-constrained welfare
    17	result depends on excluding randomised policies and is not a lower bound. The weight is chosen on the evaluation seeds,
    18	so this checks the policy class; it is not an out-of-sample estimate.
    19	
    20	**B. The Q1 explanation was wrong (your point 6).** Splitting d = g_C* - g_R3 with the gain's own normalisation into
    21	the attacked-profit and reference-profit terms shows that almost all of d is attacked profit (D2+H3: 0.019 of 0.021;
    22	X7-SC: 0.0225 of 0.024). The paper had said the rules "differ mainly in the attacker's truthful reference profit"; it
    23	now says the opposite, and labels Table 5's reference-profit row as raw profit units.
    24	
    25	**C. H4 contains D2+H3 exactly** (its author's containment note, `h4/containment.md`). The paper now calls H4 an
    26	extension of the public family written in a separate session, not a disjoint attack concept.
    27	
    28	**D. Related work.** All four references you suggested were verified from primary sources (publisher PDFs and Crossref) and added: Zarkoob, Fu, and Leyton-Brown (2020) in the introduction, as report-dependent checking that our admissible rules could use but we do not study; Wiedenbeck, Cassell, and Wellman (2014) beside the empirical-game method; Berger and Hsu (1996) for the equivalence and intersection-union logic; Morris, White, and Crowther (2019) for the calibration reporting.
    29	
    30	## Sentence corrections (your Task 1)
    31	
    32	| # | Your point | Change |
    33	|---|---|---|
    34	| 1 | Gain does not isolate the benefit of replacement | Discussion: measured against each rule's frozen reference; does not isolate the replacement benefit; would need replacement disabled |
    35	| 2 | Q1 headline omits margin, X7-SC value, search flags; "only" | Abstract and introduction: pooled, within the registered margin, both families' values, prescribed searches; "only" removed |
    36	| 3 | All Q4 passes are search-qualified | Abstract, introduction, section 9, conclusion |
    37	| 4 | "Loss-accepting" confuses relative and absolute loss | Discussion and conclusion: loss relative to the truthful reference, not necessarily negative profit |
    38	| 5 | Coalition sizes mismatched; decoy family exception | Section 9: matched two- and five-member comparisons, R3's negative worst member, decoy family 0.024 vs 0.025, no ordering claim |
